dragon-network interface configuration
======================================

Files named IFACE.conf are strict per-interface policy.  At boot,
dragon-network-boot selects the lowest-ifindex eth* interface.  A configuration
for that interface overrides /etc/dragonos/network/default.conf; an invalid or
rejected file is an error and never falls back to the default policy.  Other
interfaces remain unmanaged at boot and can be started explicitly:

    /usr/sbin/dragon-network start IFACE

The shipped /etc/dragonos/network/default.conf uses mode dhcp.  To leave the
selected primary interface unmanaged at boot, set its effective policy to mode
external: update IFACE.conf when one exists, otherwise update default.conf.

The format is data, not shell syntax.  Each non-comment line is a key followed
by one value.  Unknown keys, duplicate scalar keys, unsafe ownership/modes, and
invalid values are rejected.  dns and search are repeatable list keys.

On filesystems with Unix modes, configuration paths must be root-owned and not
group- or world-writable.  DragonOS FAT has no Unix mode bits and reports
root-owned 0777 paths; the controller accepts that exception only after verifying
the FAT filesystem magic.  The strict parser still treats configuration as data,
but FAT permissions do not isolate policy files from local writers.

DHCP:

    mode dhcp

Static IPv4:

    mode static
    address 192.0.2.10/24
    gateway 192.0.2.1
    dns 192.0.2.53
    dns 192.0.2.54
    search example.test

External ownership (unmanaged is an alias):

    mode external

Commands:

    dragon-network start IFACE
    dragon-network stop IFACE
    dragon-network renew IFACE
    dragon-network status IFACE

The service uses only /bin/busybox ip and stores private per-interface state in
/run/dragon-network/interfaces.  It derives the complete DNS view from committed
lease state and atomically replaces the regular /etc/resolv.conf file.  Static
and external/unmanaged policies do not start udhcpc.
